# Hikari Desktop - extra TLS trust anchors
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
#   Every HTTP client in the app is pinned to Conscrypt (BoringSSL), whose
#   trust anchors come from the JDK's own lib/security/cacerts snapshot - see
#   Conscrypt's Platform.getDefaultCertKeyStore(). That snapshot no longer
#   carries the legacy Comodo/Sectigo *AAA Certificate Services* root (checked
#   against Temurin 17.0.20 and 21.0.12: both have USERTrust RSA and the
#   Sectigo R46 root, neither has AAA), while GitHub's CDN still hands out
#   chains that end at a Sectigo root cross-signed by AAA. Java therefore
#   rejected the connection with
#       "Unacceptable certificate: CN=AAA Certificate Services, O=Comodo CA Limited, ..."
#   while the same machine's browser loaded the page fine - the desktop bug
#   where no extension repo would load ("the TLS handshake is being blocked by
#   this network" was our own misleading label for it).
#
#   Http.trustStore() merges three anchor sources: the JDK store, the Windows
#   certificate store (exactly what Chrome/Edge trust on the machine), and the
#   certificates below. Nothing is trusted blindly; every entry is a public CA.
#
# CONTENTS
#   * AAA Certificate Services (Comodo CA Limited), self-signed root,
#       SHA-256 d7a7a0fb5d7e2731d771e9484ebcdef71d5f0c3e0a2948782bc83ee0ea699ef4
#       valid until 2028-12-31
#     Source: https://crt.sectigo.com/AAACertificateServices.crt
#
# REGENERATING
#   curl -fsSL https://crt.sectigo.com/AAACertificateServices.crt -o AAA.crt
#   openssl x509 -inform DER -in AAA.crt -out AAA.pem   # then paste it below
#   com.hikari.app.net.TlsTrustSelfTest asserts this exact certificate is loaded
#   (and that a live GitHub raw fetch succeeds through it), so a missing or
#   wrong file fails the build instead of reaching users.
#
